# Flow Steps And Output

A `step` is one named Action or nested Flow call. It has an `action` target and
a closed `params` expression. Every node name is explicit and stable.

Run the code cells from top to bottom.

## Install Jido Action

```elixir
Mix.install([{:jido_action, "~> 3.0.0-rc.1"}])
```

## Define An Echo Action

```elixir
defmodule FlowSteps.Echo do
  use Jido.Action,
    name: "flow_steps_echo",
    schema: Zoi.map(),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  @impl true
  def run(params, _context), do: {:ok, params}
end
```

## Use The Short Form

The short form keeps one small node compact:

```elixir
defmodule FlowSteps.ShortForm do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_steps_short_form",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "echo",
      action: FlowSteps.Echo,
      params: %{value: input(:value)}
  end
end

{:ok, %{value: 3}} = Jido.Exec.run(FlowSteps.ShortForm, %{value: 3}, %{})
```

## Use The Block Form

The block form uses the same fields. Use it when a declaration is easier to
scan one field per line.

```elixir
defmodule FlowSteps.BlockForm do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_steps_block_form",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "echo" do
      action(FlowSteps.Echo)
      params(%{value: input(:value)})
    end
  end
end

{:ok, %{value: 4}} = Jido.Exec.run(FlowSteps.BlockForm, %{value: 4}, %{})
```

Short and block forms lower to the same canonical node. Do not mix keyword
fields with a `do` block in one declaration.

## Read A Prior Result

Use `result("node")` for the complete result of an earlier node. Use
`select(result("node"), path)` for one value. This reference creates the
dependency.

```elixir
defmodule FlowSteps.ResultReference do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_steps_result_reference",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "source",
      action: FlowSteps.Echo,
      params: %{value: input(:value)}

    step "copy",
      action: FlowSteps.Echo,
      params: %{value: select(result("source"), :value)}
  end
end

{:ok, %{value: 5}} = Jido.Exec.run(FlowSteps.ResultReference, %{value: 5}, %{})
```

The DSL does not assign node results to Elixir variables. Explicit names keep
the source declarative and match stored Flow data.

## Infer The Output

When `output` is absent, Jido uses the complete result of the last outer node.
Source order selects this default output only. It does not add execution
dependencies.

```elixir
%Jido.Flow{return: inferred_output} = FlowSteps.ResultReference.flow()
%Jido.Flow.Ref{type: :result, node: "copy"} = inferred_output
```

## Shape An Explicit Output

Use `output` as the final declaration when the result must combine several
sources.

```elixir
defmodule FlowSteps.ShapedOutput do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_steps_shaped_output",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "source",
      action: FlowSteps.Echo,
      params: %{value: input(:value), values: [input(:value), :constant]}

    output %{
      nested: %{value: select(result("source"), :value)},
      values: select(result("source"), :values),
      trace_id: context(:trace_id)
    }
  end
end

{:ok, %{nested: %{value: 6}, values: [6, :constant], trace_id: "trace-1"}} =
  Jido.Exec.run(FlowSteps.ShapedOutput, %{value: 6}, %{trace_id: "trace-1"})
```

A Flow must contain at least one node. `output` is optional, but if it exists,
it must be the final declaration and must stay connected to the graph.

## Add Metadata

Use `meta:` for labels, tags, notes, or tool-owned data. Metadata is
non-semantic. It does not change dependencies, execution, or identity.

```elixir
defmodule FlowSteps.Annotated do
  use Jido.Flow,
    name: "flow_steps_annotated",
    schema: Zoi.object(%{value: Zoi.integer()}),
    output_schema: Zoi.map()

  flow do
    step "echo",
      action: FlowSteps.Echo,
      params: %{value: input(:value)},
      meta: %{
        label: "Copy the value",
        tags: ["example", "mapping"],
        note: "Visible in provenance"
      }
  end
end

annotated_map = Jido.Flow.to_map(FlowSteps.Annotated.flow(), provenance: true)
[%{provenance: provenance}] = annotated_map.nodes

%{label: "Copy the value", note: "Visible in provenance"} =
  Map.take(provenance, [:label, :note])
```

Spark also records source line and column data in provenance when it is
available.

## Keep Computation In Actions

The DSL accepts references, literals, maps, and lists. It does not execute
calls such as `String.upcase/1` inside `params`. Such a declaration fails at
compile time with an unsupported Flow expression error.

Use an Action for computation. A Flow maps data between Actions.

